Here’s a step-by-step guide you can follow to reset the sa password and test it in SQL Server:

1. Stop the SQL Server service:
Open services.msc (Press Win + R, type services.msc, and press Enter).

Find SQL Server (MSSQLSERVER) (or SQL Server (SQLEXPRESS) if you're using Express).

Right-click → Stop

2. Start SQL Server in Single-User Mode:
Open Command Prompt as Administrator.

Run the following command to start SQL Server in single-user mode:

cmd
Copy
Edit
net start MSSQLSERVER /m"SQLCMD"
For a named instance (e.g., SQLEXPRESS), use:

cmd
Copy
Edit
net start MSSQL$SQLEXPRESS /m"SQLCMD"
3. Connect to SQL Server using sqlcmd:
In Command Prompt, run the following:

cmd
Copy
Edit
sqlcmd -S localhost
This should connect you to the SQL Server in single-user mode.

4. Add your Windows user to the sysadmin role:
Run these SQL commands to add your Windows login to the sysadmin role:

sql
Copy
Edit
CREATE LOGIN [CONFIGURENET\YourUsername] FROM WINDOWS;
ALTER SERVER ROLE sysadmin ADD MEMBER [CONFIGURENET\YourUsername];
GO
Replace [CONFIGURENET\YourUsername] with your actual Windows username. You can get it by running whoami in Command Prompt.

5. Exit sqlcmd:
Type the following to exit sqlcmd:

sql
Copy
Edit
EXIT
6. Restart SQL Server normally:
In services.msc, right-click SQL Server (MSSQLSERVER) → Start.

7. Log in to SQL Server using SSMS (SQL Server Management Studio):
Open SSMS.

In the Connect to Server window:

Server name: localhost (or your instance name).

Authentication: Windows Authentication.

Login: Use your Windows login (e.g., CONFIGURENET\YourUsername).

Click Connect.

8. Reset the sa password:
In SSMS, run the following SQL command to reset the sa password:

sql
Copy
Edit
ALTER LOGIN sa WITH PASSWORD = 'YourNewStrongPassword!';
9. Test the password change:
Test using SSMS:
Open SSMS again.

Authentication: SQL Server Authentication.

Login: sa.

Password: The new password you set.

If the login is successful, you're all set!

Test using sqlcmd:
Open Command Prompt and run:

cmd
Copy
Edit
sqlcmd -S localhost -U sa -P YourNewStrongPassword
If you're connected successfully, type:

sql
Copy
Edit
SELECT @@VERSION;
GO
EXIT
10. (Optional) Disable sa login for extra security:
If you don't need the sa account, disable it to enhance security:

sql
Copy
Edit
ALTER LOGIN sa DISABLE;
